From: bornaradusin <bornaradusin@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 08:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 84/84] PC5: a pointer the game moves itself stays on screen (Wine
 patch 0999)

Grim Dawn moves the Windows pointer by the controller, and PC5 2.0.8 hid
it after 5 seconds because the player hadn't moved the mouse. The game
moving the pointer to another place counts as use now.
---
 docs/WINE_PS5_BUILD.md                        |  2 +-
 ...32u-ps5-hide-the-pointer-when-unused.patch | 34 +++++++++++++------
 2 files changed, 24 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)

diff --git a/docs/WINE_PS5_BUILD.md b/docs/WINE_PS5_BUILD.md
index 730b157..b41307a 100644
--- a/docs/WINE_PS5_BUILD.md
+++ b/docs/WINE_PS5_BUILD.md
@@ -173,7 +173,7 @@ before evaluating a candidate built from that cache.
 | 0996 | `server`: on PS5, a `create_file`, rename or hard link with a root folder and an absolute unix name (what 0943 gives when it looks a name up by the folder's own name) is taken instead of failing with `STATUS_OBJECT_PATH_SYNTAX_BAD`: the unix name is opened as it is and the NT name is still made from the folder's. Heroes of Might and Magic: Olden Era got "path invalid" (`ERROR_BAD_PATHNAME`) for `StreamingAssets\maps\Story_maps` right after 0943's log line |
 | 0997 | `kernelbase`: on PS5, the listing 0988 writes for `fxc.exe` gives a `def` line's values as plain decimals (`1.0`, `-0.5`, `0.333333343`; vkd3d-shader writes `1.00000000e+00`, and `D3DXAssembleShader` takes no exponent), and a `vs_1_x` shader that fails to compile (vkd3d-shader's `E7002` for `sincos`) is compiled for `vs_2_0` instead, as Far Cry only assembles the listing again on a device that takes `vs_3_0` |
 | 0998 | `ntdll`: on PS5, a non-alertable `NtWaitForSingleObject` on an event or semaphore whose one try on the shared word (0885, 0887) fails spins up to `WINE_PS5_SYNC_SPIN_US` microseconds (100 by default, 0 off, 1000 at most; never longer than a relative timeout, none for a poll or an absolute time) re-trying the word before it blocks in the server, and stops at once when the server holds the word. While it spins the word stays the client's, so `SetEvent`/`ReleaseSemaphore` need no server call either. Blur sets six events about 2000 times a frame, each waking a worker that waits again, and each of those waits and sets cost several microseconds through the server (5 to 10 ms a frame). The log says `wine-ps5: waits spin up to N us before blocking` at the first such wait, and every 65536 spins how many took the object (`wine-ps5: wait spins: …`); `tests/test_wine_sync_spin.py` runs the spin on the actual word header |
-| 0999 | `win32u`: on PS5, the user driver hides the pointer once the player hasn't moved it or clicked for `WINE_PS5_CURSOR_IDLE_MS` milliseconds (5000 by default, 0 never), both where it draws the cursor over the screen and where Vulkan frames get it from VideoOut's hardware cursor (0740); the next move, even against an edge, or click shows it again. A game setting the cursor's position or image doesn't count, and the cursor the application set is unchanged for `GetCursorInfo`. A game played with the controller that leaves the Windows cursor on showed it, still, in a corner (Valor Mortis). |
+| 0999 | `win32u`: on PS5, the user driver hides the pointer once the player hasn't moved it or clicked for `WINE_PS5_CURSOR_IDLE_MS` milliseconds (5000 by default, 0 never), both where it draws the cursor over the screen and where Vulkan frames get it from VideoOut's hardware cursor (0740); the next move, even against an edge, or click shows it again, as does the game moving it to another place itself (`SetCursorPos`; Grim Dawn moves its pointer by the controller). A game setting the same place or a new image doesn't count, and the cursor the application set is unchanged for `GetCursorInfo`. A game played with the controller that leaves the Windows cursor on showed it, still, in a corner (Valor Mortis). |
 
 ## Allocator
 
diff --git a/wine/patches/0999-win32u-ps5-hide-the-pointer-when-unused.patch b/wine/patches/0999-win32u-ps5-hide-the-pointer-when-unused.patch
index 4881552..3b081ce 100644
--- a/wine/patches/0999-win32u-ps5-hide-the-pointer-when-unused.patch
+++ b/wine/patches/0999-win32u-ps5-hide-the-pointer-when-unused.patch
@@ -14,18 +14,20 @@ or clicked for 5 seconds, as a console's pointer fades, both where it
 draws the cursor over the screen itself and where Vulkan frames get it
 from VideoOut's hardware cursor (patch 0740). The next move (the mouse,
 the touchpad or a stick working as a mouse, even against an edge) or
-click shows it again at once. Only the player's own input counts: a game
-that sets the cursor's position or image doesn't keep it on screen.
+click shows it again at once, and so does the game moving it to another
+place itself (SetCursorPos): Grim Dawn moves its pointer by the
+controller, and a pointer the game moves is in use. A game setting the
+same place or a new image doesn't keep it on screen.
 The cursor the application set, and where it is, are unchanged, so
 GetCursorInfo and everything else still see it as shown.
 
 WINE_PS5_CURSOR_IDLE_MS sets the time in milliseconds (0 never hides it).
 ---
 diff --git a/dlls/win32u/ps5drv.c b/dlls/win32u/ps5drv.c
-index 602b922..cc5a6c9 100644
